I'm Marsha Burton, Cassini Fields and Particles Scientist at NASA's Jet Propulsion Lab.

3
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,480
Cassini has been in orbit around Saturn for more than six years,

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:22,480
and new data tell scientists that the sixth planet from the Sun is weirder than we've even imagined.

5
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:28,980
Ever since we arrived, Cassini has been measuring radio waves called Saturn Kilometric Radiation.

6
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:35,080
Cassini's radio and plasma wave instrument recently determined that the variation in radio waves

7
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:39,080
is different in the northern and southern hemispheres of Saturn.

8
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:45,080
And the northern and southern rotational variations also appear to change with its Saturnian seasons.

9
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:50,490
The radio wave patterns are controlled by the rotation of the planet,

10
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:56,490
so to Cassini, Saturn's radio waves sound a bit like bursts of a spinning air raid siren.

11
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:06,100
We can't normally hear these radio wave patterns, but Cassini scientists have translated the patterns

12
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,100
into the human audio range.

13
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:14,100
In this video, you actually hear the radio wave patterns coming from the two hemispheres

14
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:17,100
swap rates over the course of several years.

15
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:22,100
The crossover happened a few months after spring began in the northern hemisphere.

16
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:28,100
Scientists don't think the radio wave patterns indicate hemispheres actually rotating at different rates.

17
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:32,200
It has more to do with variations in high altitude winds.

18
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:37,200
A recent result from the Hubble Space Telescope also gives us clues.

19
